# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values before `docker compose up`.
# `.env` is read automatically by docker-compose.

# ── Postgres ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Postgres container is initialised with these credentials on first
# run; afterwards the same values are used to build the app's DATABASE_URL.
POSTGRES_USER=uwebia
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-strong-password
POSTGRES_DB=uwebia

# ── Flask ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app's session/token signing key. Long random string; generate with:
#   python -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))'
# It also derives the encryption key for stored credentials (Stripe/SMS/AI/OAuth),
# so CHANGING it logs everyone out and requires re-entering those in the admin UI.
# If left unset the app boots on a random, per-restart key (a loud, safe fallback).
SECRET_KEY=replace-with-64-hex-chars

# ── Trusted proxies (who is allowed to set X-Forwarded-For) ─────────────
# The app uses the client IP for the login lockout, the sign-up throttle and
# the IP recorded against forum posts. X-Forwarded-For is only a header, so it
# is believed ONLY when the machine that connected is listed here; otherwise
# the connecting address is used and the header is ignored.
#
# Accepts CIDRs and IPs, plus the keywords:
#   private     RFC1918 + loopback + link-local + IPv6 ULA   (the default)
#   cloudflare  Cloudflare's published edge ranges
#   none        trust nothing — correct when the app is exposed directly
#
# Examples:
#   Caddy/nginx sidecar or a LAN proxy ....... private          (default)
#   Cloudflare in front of HAProxy ........... 10.0.0.5,cloudflare
#   App reachable straight from the internet . none
#
# Prefer naming your proxy's exact IP over the blanket `private`: with
# `private`, anything already on your LAN can forge the header. This matters
# most if APP_HOST_PORT is published on 0.0.0.0 (see LISTEN_ADDR below).
# UWEBIA_TRUSTED_PROXIES=private

# ── Database TLS (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# By default the app probes the database once at startup: if the server
# accepts TLS it pins sslmode=require for the whole process, and if the
# server has no TLS at all it carries on but prints a loud warning. That
# closes psycopg2's default behaviour, which silently drops to plaintext
# whenever TLS isn't offered. Startup prints the mode it settled on.
#
# Set this to override the probe. Values are libpq's:
#   disable | allow | prefer | require | verify-ca | verify-full
#
#   require      encrypt, but don't check who the server is (the default
#                the probe picks) — stops passive sniffing, not MITM
#   verify-full  also authenticate the server — the real fix, but see below
#
# verify-full has two requirements that bite:
#   1. UWEBIA_DB_SSLROOTCERT must point at the CA (or the self-signed
#      server cert itself) that signed the server's certificate.
#   2. The host in your DATABASE_URL must MATCH that certificate. A cert
#      whose SAN is a DNS name cannot verify a URL written with an IP
#      address — connect by the name, and make sure it resolves. If you
#      generated a self-signed cert, give it an IP SAN or use the name.
# Check what the server presents with:
#   openssl s_client -starttls postgres -connect HOST:5432 \
#     </dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -subject -ext subjectAltName
#
# UWEBIA_DB_SSLMODE=require
# UWEBIA_DB_SSLROOTCERT=/etc/ssl/certs/uwebia-postgres-ca.crt

# ── Gunicorn (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GUNICORN_WORKERS=3
# GUNICORN_TIMEOUT=120

# ── App listener (when fronted by pfSense HAProxy / external proxy) ─────
# Host port pfSense HAProxy will forward to. Default 5772.
# APP_HOST_PORT=5772
# Set to 127.0.0.1 if the proxy is on the SAME host as docker and the
# port should NOT be reachable from the LAN. Default 0.0.0.0 (LAN-reachable).
# LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# ── Caddy (only used with `--profile caddy`) ────────────────────────────
# Ignore these if HAProxy / Nginx / Cloudflare is fronting the app instead.
#   • Real domain → Caddy provisions a Let's Encrypt cert (set ACME_EMAIL).
#   • `:80`        → Caddy serves plain HTTP on port 80 (local testing).
# SITE_ADDRESS=:80
# ACME_EMAIL=
